    "steps": [
      {
        "text": "Soak the noodles in cold water until pliable, then drain.",
        "minutes": 10
      },
      {
        "text": "Meanwhile, simmer the shrimp heads and shells in the water, crushing the heads to release their juices. Strain and measure 4 cups of stock.",
        "minutes": 15
      },
      {
        "text": "Bring the stock back to a simmer and poach the shrimp until pink. Lift them out and halve them lengthwise.",
        "minutes": null
      },
      {
        "text": "Heat the 1/4 cup oil in a skillet and fry the tofu until golden on all sides. Drain on paper towels.",
        "minutes": null
      },
      {
        "text": "Heat the 2 tablespoons oil in a saucepan over medium heat. Sauté the garlic and onion until soft. Add the stock, annatto, fish sauce, the 1/2 cup tinapa flakes and the pepper and bring to a simmer.",
        "minutes": null
      },
      {
        "text": "Whisk the flour mixture again and pour it in slowly, whisking, then simmer, stirring often, until the sauce is thick and no longer tastes of raw flour.",
        "minutes": 5
      },
      {
        "text": "Bring a large pot of water to a boil. Dip the noodles in, in batches, until tender, about 1 minute. Drain well.",
        "minutes": null
      },
      {
        "text": "Divide the noodles among plates or spread them on a platter. Ladle the sauce over and top with the shrimp, tofu, remaining tinapa, chicharon, eggs, scallions and toasted garlic. Serve with calamansi.",
        "minutes": null
      }
    ],
    "tips": "Keep the sauce and noodles separate until serving, since sauced palabok softens quickly. Leftover sauce reheats well with a splash of water."
  }
